    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i guess i'm just worried about my mindset during the actual sets. sometimes when im midway through a heavy set of squats, my brain just feels foggy or i get these weird intrusive thoughts that make me want to quit. how do i deal with that mental resistance or those distracting thoughts when the weight gets heavy?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That \"foggy brain\" feeling during heavy lifts is common and usually just signal that your body is reaching its physical limit for the day. Whenever those intrusive thoughts appear, simply remember your primary goal is to maintain technical perfection, so consciously narrow your focus to a single, manageable task like breathing rhythm or stem-feet contact. Try counting your last rep aloud or using a specific visualization technique to stay locked into form rather than the weight's heaviness. If the mental fog becomes too distracting or compromises safety, it is often wiser to check in with a coach or take the set resting longer, as consistency beats a grueling attempt with bad form.",
